# Villain Codex - mundane equipment
SOURCELONG:Villain Codex	SOURCESHORT:VC	SOURCEDATE:2017-11

Alchemical Cleaner	TYPE:Goods.General.Alchemical	COST:300	WT:1	SOURCEPAGE:p.44	DESC:This caustic alchemical solution dissolves blood, hair, and small pieces of flesh. Distributing it over a 5-foot square takes one full-round action and increases the DC of Perception checks to find such materials by 20. One pot contains enough liquid to cover nine 5-foot squares and can be thrown as a splash weapon, functioning as an alkali flask when thrown. Crafting alchemical cleaner requires a successful DC 25 Craft (alchemy) check.
Guard's Kit	TYPE:Goods.General.Kit	COST:20	WT:20	SOURCEPAGE:p.44	DESC:A standard set of supplies issued to guards. This kit consists of a backpack, a belt pouch, a flint and steel, manacles, 50 feet of rope, a signal whistle, soap, and 5 torches.

Pallid Suspension	TYPE:Goods.General.Alchemical	COST:324	WT:0	SOURCEPAGE:p.69	DESC:This small vial contains an alchemical suspension that preserves a sample of pallid gift. This functions like plague powder (Pathfinder RPG Horror Adventures 213) for pallid gift (DC 20), allowing the user to apply pallid gift to a weapon (a standard action, or whatever action the character normally takes to apply poison to a weapon if that is better) and deliver it as with an injury poison. Just as for plague powder, the creation of a pallid suspension exposes the crafter to the disease on each day of crafting.
Breathing Tube	TYPE:Goods.General	COST:1	WT:0.1	SOURCEPAGE:p.128	DESC:This simple, hollow tube roughly as long as the user's forearm is typically made to resemble a specific type of aquatic foliage, so that its user remains inconspicuous while underwater. Using a breathing tube, an air-breathing creature can stay fully submerged under water indefinitely, as long as it remains near enough to the surface of the water for the tube to reach the air.
Zip-Line Hook	TYPE:Goods.General.Tool	COST:5	WT:1	SOURCEPAGE:p.128	DESC:This blunt iron hook secures to the user's forearm with a sturdy strap. When hooked onto a rope or similar object fastened at a downward angle, the owner can climb down the rope at a speed of 30 feet as a move action instead of at her normal climbing speed, but must take at least one move action doing so each round or she falls. Using it requires a successful DC 5 Climb check for a 90-degree declination, increasing by 5 for every 15 degrees off that baseline (maximum DC 30 at a 15- or 165-degree declination). A character can use it one-handed at a -8 penalty on Climb checks. If the zip line or its rope breaks while in use, the character falls. A masterwork zip-line hook (55 gp) grants a +2 circumstance bonus on Climb checks to descend a rope. An improvised zip-line hook takes 1d6 damage from friction each move action.
Loaded Shell Game Cups	TYPE:Goods.General.Tool	COST:10	WT:1	SOURCEPAGE:p.236	DESC:This set of three cups and accompanying balls is designed for cheating at the popular shell game. A small lever hidden on the base of each cup flips an extra chamber back and forth: when open the ball is visible, and when shut the cup scoops the ball inside and hides it. The dealer can control which cup reveals a ball while keeping the others hidden. To do this, the dealer must succeed at a Sleight of Hand check with a +5 bonus, opposed by the mark's Perception check, to activate the levers without being noticed.
Quick-Pack Table	TYPE:Goods.General	COST:5	WT:20	SOURCEPAGE:p.236	DESC:A quick-pack table is a piece of lightweight furniture designed to be both portable and versatile. Street-corner hawkers commonly use such tables to display small goods for sale. It can be set up in a relatively small area, usually taking up a few square feet, and has four legs, a flat top surface, and a bookcase-style upright display. The table and its contents can be closed, folded up, and collapsed in a single round with all goods safely held in their compartments, and the owner can carry the folded table under one arm.
Shoddy Item	TYPE:Goods.General	COST:0	SOURCEPAGE:p.116	DESC:Rule (requires the Craft Shoddy Item feat). Price and weight vary (same as a normal item of its kind; sold for the same price as well-made counterparts). A shoddy item is any mundane piece of equipment cleverly but poorly crafted so its materials and workmanship are worse than expected, with the flaws concealed. Shoddy items are never masterwork or made of special materials, and single-use items can never be shoddy. A shoddy item has its hardness reduced by 2 and its hit points reduced by half. A shoddy weapon takes the damage it deals each attack (minus its lower hardness); shoddy armor takes damage identical to its wearer's hit-point loss from a successful attack (after adjusted hardness). Other shoddy items have a 10% chance to gain the broken condition each day of use, and if they have limited uses, they have half as many. Broken shoddy items can never be repaired without further Craft checks and gp expenditure; destroyed shoddy items cannot be recovered. A DC 15 Appraise (or appropriate Craft) check notices the shoddy condition, but only on specific inspection.
Caravan Master's Wagon	TYPE:Goods.General	COST:1000	WT:2000	SOURCEPAGE:p.116	DESC:Similar in most respects to a high-quality heavy smuggler's wagon, but it additionally allows for a hidden portable hole (price not included) secreted within it. The portable hole rests on the wagon bed with a second layer of wooden planks laid atop it and fastened with nuts and bolts so they appear to be a normal part of the wagon, sitting between the layers of wood like meat between two slices of bread. Those who know can fill it with illicit goods, fasten the decking, and load normal cargo atop it; the wagon weighs no more than it should. The planks are spaced far enough apart to admit breathable air, allowing transport of live cargo. A successful DC 30 Perception check is necessary to discover the hidden second layer.
Smuggler's Wagon	TYPE:Goods.General	COST:0	SOURCEPAGE:p.116	DESC:Price varies (see table); weight is the same as the original wagon. Functions in most respects exactly like an ordinary wagon (Pathfinder RPG Ultimate Equipment 87), except it contains hidden compartments that allow cargo to be transported in secret. Price depends on the size/type of the original wagon and the skill of the hidden compartments (the number in parentheses is the Perception DC to locate them during a search): Light -- Low (DC 15) 75 gp, Med. (DC 20) 100 gp, High (DC 25) 150 gp; Medium -- 115 gp / 150 gp / 225 gp; Heavy -- 150 gp / 200 gp / 300 gp. Compartments can be located throughout the wagon (hollowed-out axle, beneath seats, between floor panels, and so forth). The total weight the wagon and its draft animals can accommodate remains the same as the original.
Sucker's Barrel	TYPE:Goods.General	COST:4	WT:35	SOURCEPAGE:p.117	DESC:Constructed to appear to the casual eye exactly like a normal barrel. An inner barrier installed near one of the barrel's ends hides a cavity containing a bladder filled with water. This creates a false bottom such that, when the barrel is full, it weighs and feels the same as a barrel filled completely with a more valuable liquid such as alcohol or oil. In this way a merchant can fill a barrel with a smaller quantity of a liquid (typically 75% of the barrel's total capacity) and then sell it as if it were a full barrel's worth.
Cutlass	TYPE:Weapon.Melee.Martial	COST:15	WT:4	DAMAGE:1d6	CRITRANGE:3	CRITMULT:x2	SPROP:Slashing (S).	SOURCEPAGE:p.188	DESC:A cutlass is a short, curved sword with a single sharp edge. Pirates use cutlasses because the blade is keen enough to cut through rigging while small enough to use in crowded shipboard melees. One-handed martial melee weapon; damage 1d4 (Small) / 1d6 (Medium), critical 18-20/x2, slashing.
Hook Hand	TYPE:Weapon.Melee.Simple	COST:10	WT:1	DAMAGE:1d4	CRITRANGE:1	CRITMULT:x2	SPROP:Slashing (S). Disarm. Cannot be disarmed.	SOURCEPAGE:p.188	DESC:Pirates who lose a hand sometimes attach hooks to their wrists. Hook hands can't be disarmed. They generally don't interfere with day-to-day activities, but can't be used for tasks that would require an actual hand (such as completing somatic components, drawing arrows, or holding a weapon with both hands). One-handed simple melee weapon; damage 1d3 (Small) / 1d4 (Medium), critical x2, slashing, disarm.
Dragoon Pistol	TYPE:Weapon.Ranged.Firearm.Exotic	COST:1500	WT:5	DAMAGE:1d8	CRITRANGE:1	CRITMULT:x3	RANGE:30	SPROP:Early firearm (one-handed). Misfire 1 (5 ft.). Capacity 3. Damage type B and P. Damage 1d6 (Small) / 1d8 (Medium).	SOURCEPAGE:p.57	DESC:Like the dragoon musket, this long-barreled pistol is more precise than a typical pistol but slightly less deadly on a critical hit. It can fire only dragoon cartridges or specially modified mage shot.
Dragoon Musket	TYPE:Weapon.Ranged.Firearm.Exotic	COST:2000	WT:12	DAMAGE:1d12	CRITRANGE:1	CRITMULT:x3	RANGE:60	SPROP:Early firearm (two-handed). Misfire 1-2 (5 ft.). Capacity 3. Damage type B and P. Damage 1d10 (Small) / 1d12 (Medium).	SOURCEPAGE:p.57	DESC:This long-barreled musket shoots more precisely than other muskets but is slightly less damaging on a critical hit. It can fire only dragoon cartridges or specially modified mage shot.
Dragoon Cartridge	TYPE:Weapon.Ranged.Firearm.Ammunition	COST:30	SPROP:Ammunition. Weight not specified in the text.	SOURCEPAGE:p.57	DESC:These clips of alchemical cartridges hold three bullets and fit the slot of either the dragoon musket or the dragoon pistol, allowing the wielder to fire up to three shots in quick succession without reloading. Once the cartridge is spent, reloading another dragoon cartridge takes a full-round action regardless of firearm type. Rapid Reload can reduce the action time, but abilities like the musket master's fast musket have no effect. Unlike other alchemical cartridges, dragoon cartridges don't increase a dragoon firearm's misfire chance by 1. Dragoon cartridges can be used only with dragoon firearms.
